{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "The Starting Point of Minicomputers", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 9, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 2, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A1.1", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words you use to talk about microcomputers.", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Identify information about the history of software and its characteristics in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Understands key information in simple written and spoken texts about topics related to software programming and its history.  Recognizes basic ideas on programming concepts and programming history in both written and spoken passages.", "type": "secondary_1"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le02ob09re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Simple past tense of \"verb be\", \"were\" in questions", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "We can use the <span class= \"strong\">  simple past tense  </span> of the <span class= \"strong\"> verb to be </span> for asking questions about the state of things or people in the past.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "When we want to use the simple past tense of the verb <strong>be</strong> for asking questions, we use the following structure:&nbsp;<br /><br /><strong>Verb Be in Simple Past</strong>&nbsp;(Was/&nbsp;<strong>Were</strong>) + Subject + Complement + Question mark (?)<br /> Example:&nbsp;<strong><i>Were</i></strong><strong>&nbsp;</strong><strong><i>microcomputers</i></strong><i> an important invention?</i><br /> <br /> Remember that when we ask a question, we place the verb before the subject.&nbsp;<br /> <br /> When we have an information question, we place the interrogative adverb <strong>before the verb</strong> <strong>be</strong>. Let's consider the following example:&nbsp;<br /> <br /> Example:&nbsp;<strong><i> How were</i></strong><i>&nbsp;</i><i>the first microcomputers?</i>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Why were microcomputers a good idea?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "How were these new computers innovative?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Were these minicomputers used only by scientists and engineers?", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Verb Can in Questions", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the modal verb <b>can</b> to talk about possibility, ability, requests or permission. Here, we'll focus on the use of this modal verb in questions. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Take a look at the following question:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<center><i><strong>Can</strong> you <strong>read</strong> that paragraph about microcomputers?", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Notice that the structure of this question is similar to the one explained in the previous Learning Point. You place the verb <strong>can</strong> before the subject and then, you place the verb.</br></br>  It's important to say that the verb can remains the same regardless of the subject of the sentence. Besides, the main verb of the sentence stays in its base form. As you can see, the verb in the example is <strong>read</strong> and it takes the base form.</br></br>   Now, in terms of meaning, notice that in the question we use the modal verb <strong>can</strong> to indicate <strong>a request or an instruction.</strong></br></br>   Take a look at the following examples:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<i><strong>Can</strong> you <strong>help</strong> me understand the functioning of microcomputers? (Request)</br> <strong>Can</strong> I <strong>read</strong> your text about ENIAC? (Request)", "type": "text"}, {"content": "It is also possible to use <strong>information questions</strong> when we use the verb <strong>can</strong> in interrogative sentences, just follow the structure below:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<center><strong>Interrogative adverb</strong> (What, How, Where, Why, etc.) + <strong>Modal verb can</strong> + Subject + Complement + Question mark (?)", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Example: <i><strong>How can</strong> microcomputers change the economy?</i> (Ability)", "type": "text"}, {"content": "How can we make computers more affordable?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "How can integrated circuits make computers smaller?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Can a solution like this make a real difference?", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "afford", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to have enough money or capital to buy something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: After that, scientists and engineers were able to afford computers. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le02ob09aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "however ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "word used to contrast two ideas or statements", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(connector)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: However, some people in the industry thought that computers didn't need to be so exclusive.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le02ob09aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "decrease ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to diminish", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The prices of microcomputers decreased and by 1970 there were around 50 companies making microcomputers.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le02ob09aud03", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "room ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "synonym for space ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: As more transistors fit in a smaller space, computer memory, logic circuits and other components take less room, and computers become smaller.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le02ob09aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "transistorized", "type": "name"}, {"content": "something that is equipped or that contains transistors", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Their solution was to replace the vacuum tubes with transistorized models for making smaller and cheaper computers. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le02ob09aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "economies of scale ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the inverse relationship that occurs when the quantity of a product increases and its price decreases", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Thanks to the new technology and the economies of scale, the prices of microcomputers decreased.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le02ob09aud06", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Make a list of important facts about the origins of minicomputers. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Take notes of some crucial dates and moments in the evolution of minicomputers in the industry. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "c)\tUse all the information you have to talk about the microcomputer. Be ready to ask and answer questions.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"content": "I list important facts about the origins of minicomputers.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I recognize some important moments in the evolution of minicomputers in the industry.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I prepare a presentation for my classmates and instructor.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "templa